journal={Frontiers in Marine Science},
language={English},
keywords={Earth Sciences--Oceanography; Cladocopium; Climate Change; dinoflagellates; Nutrients; seagrass; Sedimentation; Syndiniales; Carbon dioxide; Ammonium; Dinoflagellates; Chlorophylls; High temperature; Phytoplankton; Symbionts; Chlorophyll; Coral reefs; Aquatic microorganisms; Algae; Acidification; Water quality; Ammonium compounds; Mesocosms; Benthos; Experiments; Primary production; Seawater; Sea grass; Salinity; Dinoflagellata; United States--US; Taiwan},
abstract={The effects of seagrass on microalgal assemblages under experimentally elevated temperatures (28oC) and CO2 partial pressures (pCO2; 800 atm) were examined using coral reef mesocosms. Concentrations of nitrate, ammonium, and benthic microalgal chlorophyll a (chl-a) were significantly higher in seagrass mesocosms, whereas phytoplankton chl-a concentrations were similar between seagrass and seagrass-free control mesocosms. In the seagrass group, fewer parasitic dinoflagellate OTUs (e.g., Syndiniales) were found in the benthic microalgal community though more symbiotic dinoflagellates (e.g., Cladocopium spp.) were quantified in the phytoplankton community. Our results suggest that, under ocean acidification conditions, the presence of seagrass nearby coral reefs may 1) enhance benthic primary productivity, 2) decrease parasitic dinoflagellate abundance, and 3) increase the presence of symbiotic dinoflagellates.},
